### Splatting a scope into lookup

Every scope keeps two extra fallback places identifier lookup checks, *after* its own declarations: they never override anything already reachable, and once resolution moves past a scope it keeps going outward, scope by scope, to Global.

`@splatr x` adds `x` as a **read-only** fallback -- its members resolve unprefixed, but nothing writes through it. `@splat x` adds it as a **writable** fallback: a plain write (`=`, `+=`, ...) to a name the scope already has lands on that member instead of declaring a fresh local. `@unsplat x` takes it back out.
###
Raft {
	length := 0
	crew := 0
}

r := Raft()
r.length = 12
r.crew = 4

@splatr r
@assert length == 12, 'a splatted-in scope resolves its members with no r. prefix'
@unsplat r
# `length` here would now raise Disk::Undeclared_Identifier -- r is no longer in lookup


### In a function param list

`@splat` / `@splatr` on a param unpacks that argument for the whole body -- the common reason to splat, and where the shorthand earns its keep:
###
Vector {
	x := 0
	y := 0
}

magnitude ( @splatr vec;
	(x ** 2 + y ** 2).sqrt()
)

v := Vector()
v.x = 3
v.y = 4
@assert magnitude(v) == 5.0, '@splatr unpacks the argument so x / y resolve directly in the body'


### Type-checking a splatted param

A `@splat` / `@splatr` param can carry a type annotation, and unlike a plain param annotation it's *enforced* at the call. The annotation names the members the body reaches unprefixed, so the wrong shape has to fail right here -- otherwise it only surfaces later as a confusing `Disk::Undeclared_Identifier` deep in the body.

A *nominal* annotation (`: Vector`) means the argument has to actually be a `Vector`, or compose one:
###
Coordinate {
	x := 0
	y := 0
}

nominal_magnitude ( @splatr vec: Vector;
	(x ** 2 + y ** 2).sqrt()
)

@assert nominal_magnitude(v) == 5.0, 'a real Vector satisfies the : Vector annotation'

# nominal_magnitude(Coordinate())  -- raises Disk::Type_Contract_Violation: expected Vector not
#                                     Coordinate, even though Coordinate happens to have x and y too


### A structural annotation checks shape, not name

`@splatr vec: <x: Number, y: Number>` is the duck-typing form -- it accepts *any* value carrying an `x` and a `y`, whatever that value's own type is:
###
structural_magnitude ( @splatr vec: <x: Number, y: Number>;
	(x ** 2 + y ** 2).sqrt()
)

c := Coordinate()
c.x = 6
c.y = 8
@assert structural_magnitude(c) == 10.0, 'a Coordinate satisfies <x: Number, y: Number> without being a Vector'
@assert structural_magnitude(v) == 5.0, 'and a Vector satisfies it too -- shape is all that matters'

# structural_magnitude(nil)  -- raises Disk::Type_Contract_Violation: nil has neither member


### Writes land on the splatted instance

`@splat` (writable) redirects a write to a name the instance already has straight onto that instance. Declaring a fresh name with `:=` always stays local either way -- only a write to an existing member gets redirected:
###
Compass {
	x := 0
	y := 0
}

double_bearing ( @splat vec;
	x *= 2
	y *= 2
	vec
)

cv := Compass()
cv.x = 3
cv.y = 4
doubled := double_bearing(cv)
@assert doubled.x == 6 && doubled.y == 8, '@splat routes the writes back onto the argument'


### Stays visible to functions declared afterward, even nested ones

A splatted scope is just another place lookup checks, not a one-time substitution -- so its members stay reachable from any function declared later in that scope, including one nested inside another:
###
Camp {
	survivors := 0
	water := 0

	Self ( survivors, water;
		self.survivors = survivors
		self.water = water
	)
}

ration_report (;
	c := Camp(12, 40)
	@splatr c

	summarize (;
		"`survivors` survivors, `water` units of water"
	)

	summarize()
)

@assert ration_report() == '12 survivors, 40 units of water', 'a nested function still sees the splatted scope'


### Lookup order: own declarations, then writable, then read-only

When a name exists in more than one place the priority is `[self, writable, read-only]` -- for reads *and* writes. An own declaration always wins, even over a same-named member reachable through a writable splat:
###
Marker { val := 0 }

val := 100
w := Marker()
w.val = 1
ro := Marker()
ro.val = 2

@splat w
@splatr ro

@assert val == 100, "the own declaration wins, not w's or ro's"
val = 999
@assert val == 999 && w.val == 1, 'the write lands on the own declaration too, never redirects through w'


### Writable beats read-only when nothing owns the name

With no own `y` in the way, a name reachable through both resolves through the writable splat:
###
Marker2 { y := 0 }

w2 := Marker2()
w2.y = 10
ro2 := Marker2()
ro2.y = 20

@splat w2
@splatr ro2

@assert y == 10, 'no own declaration, so writable wins over read-only'


### Weak references, not strong ones

Splatting something in doesn't keep it alive -- it's a *weak* reference. Once nothing else refers to it, it's free to be garbage collected even though it's technically still "in", so `@unsplat` is only for cutting something off early, not for avoiding a leak. This matters most for long-lived scopes -- the top level of a program, or anything a running `@start_server` reuses across requests.
###
tmp := Vector()
tmp.x = 3
tmp.y = 4
@assert magnitude(tmp) == 5.0, 'no @unsplat needed after the call -- the weak reference just goes away'


### The standard library works this way too

`String` / `Array` / etc. are reachable through Global's own read-only splat, not declared on Global directly -- see demos/loading_code.disk. Reassigning a built-in name shadows it for the rest of the program without touching the real one; if the replacement still composes the original, every literal keeps working and picks up whatever else you added:
###
Mine | Array { extra := true }
Array = Mine

@assert [1,2,3].length() == 3, 'reassigning Array keeps literals working -- Mine composes Array'
@assert [1].extra == true, 'every array literal now picks up what Mine added'


### Global -- the scope keyword

`Global` is a bare scope keyword, the same shape as `self` and `Self`: alone it's the global scope as a value, and `Global.name` reaches a name on it specifically -- past any local shadow, without an outward search:
###
Global.tally := 0

count_up ( n;
	tally := 999          # a fresh local shadow -- doesn't touch the global
	Global.tally = Global.tally + n
)

count_up(5)
count_up(3)
@assert Global.tally == 8, 'Global. reaches the global member, never the local shadow'
@assert tally == 8, 'and bare `tally` finds it too, once nothing local is in the way'


### Reopening a scope directly

`@push_scope <Type or instance>` pushes that scope onto the stack, so declarations made while it's active become real members of the target -- unlike a splat, which only adds a lookup fallback without mutating anything. `@pop_scope <same target>` pops back out, asserting the target matches what was pushed.
###
Button {
	label := 'default'
}

@push_scope Button
css_filter := 'invert()'
@pop_scope Button

b := Button()
@assert b.css_filter == 'invert()', 'a member declared while pushed onto the Type shows up on an instance'


### Reopening a Type extends every instance

`css_filter` went onto the `Button` type, so every `Button()` -- built before or after -- sees it, exactly as if it were in the original body:
###
another_button := Button()
@assert another_button.css_filter == 'invert()', 'the declaration is shared by every Button'


### Reopening one instance changes just that instance

`@push_scope` also takes an instance directly -- then the new declarations land only on that one value:
###
button_c := Button()
button_d := Button()

@push_scope button_c
onclick := 'clicked'
@pop_scope button_c

@assert button_c.onclick == 'clicked', 'the reopened instance has the new member'
# button_d.onclick would raise Disk::Undeclared_Identifier -- only button_c was reopened, not the type
